{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak to the children of Israel.</b> One needs to know why it was not sufficient with what preceded it, namely <b>לֵאמֹר</b>, for the matter is understood that he was to say it to the children of Israel — or else it should not have needed to say ״לֵאמֹר״ and could have sufficed with saying ״Speak to the children of Israel.״" "And it appears to me according to what the Sages, z״l, said (Yoma 4a): From where do we know that one who says a matter to his fellow is subject to the prohibition of ״do not say״ (i.e., do not relay it further)? As it is stated: ״וַיְדַבֵּר HaShem אֶל מֹשֶׁה לֵאמֹר.״ And according to these things it becomes necessary to say ״Speak to the children of Israel,״ for otherwise Moses would think that He came only to grant permission — and if this is not subject to ״do not say,״ from where is it an obligation? Scripture therefore says ״Speak״ etc. And according to this approach you will find rest for your soul throughout the entire Torah, wherever Scripture says ״לֵאמֹר״ and then goes back to say ״Speak,״ for in every commandment it is necessary to say to him both ״לֵאמֹר״ and ״Speak,״ since with either one alone it would not be heard that the matter is an obligation upon him to say to them, but rather mere permission. And our Sages, z״l (Yalkut HaReubeni), expounded ״Speak to the children of Israel״ — that he should not count the mixed multitude among them, and they interpret the language as denoting authority and dominion. It will further be explained nearby in another manner." "<b>And they shall take for Me.</b> One needs to know why it wrote a vav at the beginning of the matter. Perhaps it intends according to the statement of the Sages, may their memory be for a blessing (Shekalim 1:3), that they exact the shekel dues, and they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Tanhuma), that three offerings are mentioned here: two of them are shekels, a bekha per head — one for the sockets and one for the communal offerings — and the third is the donation for the Mishkan up to this point. According to this, the verse intended by saying <b>and they shall take</b> to correspond to the donation of shekels alluded to in the offering adjacent to it, meaning that it is subject to compulsory weighing. And it attached the word <b>and they shall take</b> to ״the children of Israel״ to hint that regarding the shekels Scripture is speaking, for none weigh in except the children of Israel and not the daughters of Israel, as it is written (Exodus 30:13): ״כֹּל הָעוֹבֵר״ etc. And accordingly it said <b>from et every man</b>, meaning that the taking should be on equal terms from et every man, and even that which his heart prompts him [to give] should be an equal taking from all of them, and the generous person shall not increase nor the miser decrease. Further, the added vav intends to hint that another matter had already preceded, and it is the willing heart, as alluded to at the end of His words where He said <b>whose heart prompts him</b>, and according to this approach Scripture is also speaking about the donation for the Mishkan, and HaShem desires that every donation be from the heart and that the giving not occur until the willing impulse has first preceded in the heart." "<b>Further,</b> it appears to explain Scripture according to what they said in tractate Bava Batra (8a): A community fund is collected by two and distributed by three; it is collected by two because one does not appoint authority over the public with fewer than two. And they said there: From where are these matters derived? Scripture says (Exodus 28:5): ׳וְהֵם יִקְחוּ׳. And what is authority? That they exact charity if it is due — end of quotation. And the Tosafot and the Ran raised there that the two they mentioned are specifically for a fixed matter, but for something not fixed, three are required. And know that regarding matters of judgment, even a single individual, if he is an expert, can compel a person against his will to be judged by him alone, as it is taught (Sanhedrin 5a): If a single individual is an expert for the public etc., he may judge alone. And the Tosafot wrote there: And he may compel the person against his will, for if he were to accept him etc. From this I learn that the same holds all the more so — that he may exercise sole authority and does not need two; and just as a matter requiring three judges may judge and compel, authority that suffices with two — all the more so! And through this we will gain understanding of the verse: His saying <b>Speak to the children of Israel</b> does not mean speech but rather authority and dominion, meaning that he alone may perform the two functions that exist in the matter: the first is assessment, and the second is authority. For even though assessment requires three and authority requires two as mentioned, you alone shall have authority over the matter, because you are a single expert and will suffice in place of three, and all the more so in place of two regarding authority — and specifically you, but as for everyone else, <b>and they shall take</b> is in the plural. And you will find that this is indeed what is written (Exodus 36:3): ״וַיִּקְחוּ מִלִּפְנֵי מֹשֶׁה״ — see that Moses alone received the donation, whether for something that has a fixed amount or for something that has no fixed amount. And although the Talmud derived this from the verse (Exodus 28:5) ״וְהֵם יִקְחוּ,״ perhaps there it innovated regarding the matter of authority, which requires two, whereas here, regarding the specifics of taking something that has no fixed amount, it did not grant permission to do so with a single individual except for Moses — but anyone else requires a plurality. And according to this approach, His saying <b>and they shall take</b> with the added vav is justified, in that He first stated the authority of Moses, and therefore it is fitting to say afterward ״and they shall take.״" "<b>From et every man etc.</b> Perhaps it intends to say that regarding a man about whom it is known that he is generous of heart, they shall not subject him to the rule of assessment to determine what they will receive from him. Since he is presumed to have a generous heart, they shall take from him whatever he brings, for he himself surely knows that this is his capacity, and this is the contribution I am obligated to give — as He says <b>et My contribution.</b>" "It further intends to say that it is not considered worthy of being called ״terumati״ (My offering) except that which comes from the voluntary generosity of a person's heart, and that is what it says — <b>from every man whose</b> etc. — <b>you shall take et terumati</b>; but that which is [collected by obligation] it does not call ״terumati״ but rather ״terumah״ (an offering), as it is written: ״וְיִקְחוּ לִי תְּרוּמָה״ (״and they shall take for Me an offering״)." "There further appears to be a fine point to examine in its saying these three inclusions — <b>from every man</b> — for it should have said only ״from every one whose heart prompts him״ etc. Perhaps the verse intends to include three specific categories that have a special status regarding the laws of charity for all Israel, and they are: orphans, women, and the poor. Orphans — as they said in tractate Bava Batra (8a): charity collectors are not appointed over orphans, but if it is to make a name for them, it is permitted. Women — as they said at the end of tractate Kamma (119a): that one takes from women only a small amount, and they further said there that 'small' and 'large' are relative to what the women themselves are [worth], as in the deed of Ravina who took gold chains from them and said that relative to the people of Mechoza it is a small thing. The poor — as they said in Bava Batra (there), that charity collectors are forbidden to demand of him and collect from him etc., and a story is brought there (Ta'anit 24a) further, that the collectors would slip away from appearing before him, lest he feel constrained and give what he does not have the strength to give. Now, in the donation for the Tabernacle, HaShem said to Moses that he should take from all three of the aforementioned, and that is what it multiplied in the three inclusions: orphans, women, the poor. <b>et</b> — to include the women; <b>kol</b> ('every') — to include the orphans; <b>a man whose heart prompts him</b> — this is the poor, in its plain sense; that from all three of them he should take even a large amount." "<b>And there is</b> a reason for this matter according to the words of the Sages, may their memory be for a blessing (Tanchuma), who said that the Tabernacle serves as atonement for the sin of the golden calf. They further said (Exodus Rabbah 33:8), and this is their language: Moses said before HaShem, 'Can Israel really build a Tabernacle?' He said to him: 'From the smallest among them' etc., for the manna used to bring down precious stones and pearls for Israel — end of quote. And they also said (Tanchuma) that from the spoil of Egypt, even the smallest of them took a load of forty donkeys of silver and gold and precious stones etc. And they said (Shir HaShirim Rabbah 1:11) further that the plunder of the sea was greater than the plunder of Egypt — see, then, that Israel possessed extraordinary wealth. According to this, there is a sound reason for all three of them, for they did not say 'one does not take from orphans' except in a case where there is no [establishment of a] name and no atonement involved; but in a case where one of those elements is present, as with the Tabernacle — one does take from them. And women too, in a case of atonement, one takes from them; and furthermore, even if they give a large amount, relative to their extraordinary wealth it is accounted as a small thing. And the poor too, on account of the abundance of wealth, whatever he gives for the Tabernacle donation will make no impression even if he gives everything required for the Tabernacle. And it is precise in saying <b>you shall take et terumati</b>, to say that He did not permit taking from the aforementioned categories — <b>from every man</b> etc. — except <b>et terumati</b>, which is the offering of the Tabernacle, for the reasons we have explained." "And by way of remez (homiletic allusion), it intends that each person should aim to cleave to HaShem the aspect of his soul called terumah, through the medium of action, and that is what it says: <b>and they shall take for Me a terumah</b>, for the soul of Israel is called terumah, as it is written (Jeremiah 2:3): ״קֹדֶשׁ יִשְׂרָאֵל לַה׳ רֵאשִׁית תְּבוּאָתוֹ״ (״Israel is holy to HaShem, the first of His produce״), and terumah is called reishit (the first). And through the medium of taking from every man etc., they will attain that the Shekhinah rests upon them, and that is what it says: <b>you shall take et terumati</b>, for the Shekhinah is called terumat HaShem (the offering of HaShem), and the discerning one will understand."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {2 "The source reference '(תנחומא)' after 'may their memory be for a blessing' appears to mark a new citation distinct from the preceding Shekalim reference; rendered as given.", 5 "The contrast is between an offering called 'terumati' ('My offering,' indicating special Divine intimacy through voluntary giving) and one called merely 'terumah' (a standard obligatory offering); the translation preserves both Hebrew terms to maintain the distinction."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:34:00.751463203Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}